wledged the restart, observed a different boot ID, and revalidated service, listener, firewall, data, authentication, CRUD, and lock behavior. Fixed lifecycle boot identities independently agree with the transition.","evidence_ids":["cmd-15cbf14da1154073","cmd-4b02a8d3036a4842","cmd-630de76896e14d80","cmd-764f696fa5f14024","cmd-be6538eb2fe14e76","cmd-010f4db408fd4378","cmd-a3be3774adf4458f","cmd-75add3bbcdd14f9a","global:after_prepare:node1:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied. The WebDAV workspace is operational on port 8080 with authenticated editor CRUD, effective exclusive-lock enforcement, anonymous denial, preserved source documents, and an unchanged RHEL release and kernel. The mandatory node-restart protocol is complete and coherent, with full functional recovery after the boot identity changed.","limitations":["Several sensitive locking and authentication result values were redacted from captured stdout. Successful explicit assertions, content comparisons, cleanup results, and zero exit status still provide strong behavioral evidence.","No final checksum was captured for access.txt itself; preservation is supported by unchanged metadata and by the command timeline showing only reads of that file.","HTTP behavior was tested from node1 through its managed IP rather than from a separately captured remote client. The wildcard listener and firewalld state support network availability but do not constitute an independent off-node request."]}
